   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The next step in the [\"Transient populations\"](https://posydon.org/POSYDON/latest/tutorials-examples/population-synthesis/bbh_analysis.html) tutorial is to build a selection function. There, we basically need to construct a pandas dataframe which contains only the information that we need for our further analysis. We will build this step by step, as in the tutorial.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the later calculations we are gonna do, it is convenient to switch from an S1 / S2 notation to donor / accretor notation. To do that, we will need in the selection function to loop over the systems and check which one is the compact object (BH or NS) and assign that to be the accretor, while the other star will be the donor. Unlike a transient-event selection, this snapshot calculation only needs the final state in `oneline`; the function keeps the `history_chunk` argument so it\n",
    "can use POSYDON's standard population-selection interface."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b79ed2e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def XRB_selection_function(history_chunk, oneline_chunk,\n",
    "                           formation_channels_chunk=None):\n",
    "    '''Create a snapshot table with basic donor/accretor properties.'''\n",
    "    indices = oneline_chunk.index.to_numpy()\n",
    "    df_XRBs = pd.DataFrame(index=indices)\n",
    "\n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"time\"] = oneline_chunk[\"time_f\"] * 1.0e-6  # Myr\n",
    "    #This is not really necessary in our case, since we have one file per metallicity, \n",
    "    #but it is required by the functionality of the \"transient population\" we will use\n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"metallicity\"] = oneline_chunk[\"metallicity\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "    compact_types = {\"BH\", \"NS\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Identify which star is the compact-object accretor.\n",
    "    s1 = oneline_chunk[\"S1_state_f\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "    s2 = oneline_chunk[\"S2_state_f\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "    is1 = np.isin(s1, list(compact_types))\n",
    "    is2 = np.isin(s2, list(compact_types))\n",
    "    one_compact = is1 ^ is2            # exactly one compact star -> valid XRB\n",
    "    accretor_is_1 = one_compact & is1  # the compact object is S1\n",
    "\n",
    "    def pick(v1, v2, fill=np.nan):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Picks v1 where S1 is a compact object and v2 if S2 is a compact object.\"\"\"\n",
    "        return np.where(one_compact,             # outer True branch\n",
    "                        np.where(accretor_is_1,\n",
    "                                 v1,         # inner True  (v1 is accretor)\n",
    "                                 v2),        # inner False (v2 is accretor)\n",
    "                        fill)                    # outer False branch (not exactly one compact object)\n",
    "\n",
    "    m1 = oneline_chunk[\"S1_mass_f\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "    m2 = oneline_chunk[\"S2_mass_f\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Note the reverse m1 and m2 order for donor vs accretor!    \n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"donor_mass\"] = pick(m2, m1)\n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"accretor_mass\"] = pick(m1, m2)\n",
    "\n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"donor_state\"] = pick(s2, s1, fill=None)\n",
    "    df_XRBs[\"accretor_state\"] = pick(s1, s2, fill=None)\n",
    "\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return df_XRBs\n"
   ]
  },
